#!markdown

# Marey — a tour in a notebook

A plotting library where **the units are part of the type**. `t [s]` and `x [m]` on the axes below
were not typed by hand; they are derived from the unit witnesses the data carries, and the figure
would not have compiled had the two series disagreed about what a metre is.

Everything in this notebook runs against the packages as published. Nothing is configured.

> **On the format.** This is a `.dib`, not an `.ipynb`: plain text, one cell per `#!` directive and
> no embedded output, so it diffs as text. VS Code and `dotnet repl` open it directly; GitHub does
> not render it.

#!fsharp

#r "nuget: Marey.Html"

open Marey
open Marey.UnitsOfMeasure

#!markdown

One reference: Core, Units, the SVG backend and Plot all arrive transitively.

For the figures below to appear as pictures rather than as field tables, install the **Marey**
extension from Verso's Extensions panel once. That is a separate step from the `#r`: an extension
is executable code that runs inside the notebook host, so Verso asks for approval, per version.
Without it everything still runs, and the objects are shown instead of the pictures.
`docs/notebooks/README.md` has the details.

## The headline

Free fall. The `x` array is `float<m>` because `0.5 * 9.81<m/s^2> * t * t` is, and the axis label
follows from that and from nothing else.

#!fsharp

let t = Array.init 24 (fun i -> float i * 0.4<s>)
let x = t |> Array.map (fun t -> 0.5 * 9.81<m/s^2> * t * t)

Chart.lineIn U.second U.metre t x
|> Chart.withName "model"
|> Chart.withTitle "Free fall"
|> Chart.withSubtitle "displacement against elapsed time"
|> Chart.withLegend LegendTopLeft
|> Chart.withGrid true true

#!markdown

No `.Show()`, no `display`, no save-then-open. The cell's value *is* a `Figure`, and a `Figure`
renders as SVG.

Add a measured series to the same panel and the legend, the domain union and the colour cycle all
follow:

#!fsharp

let measured = t.[..5] |> Array.map (fun t -> 0.5 * 9.6<m/s^2> * t * t)

Chart.lineIn U.second U.metre t x
|> Chart.withName "model"
|> Chart.addScatterIn U.second U.metre t.[..5] measured
|> Chart.withName "measured"
|> Chart.withTitle "Free fall"
|> Chart.withLegend LegendTopLeft
|> Chart.withGrid true true

#!markdown

## What the type system is actually doing

Displacement and temperature on the same axis: both are `float`s at runtime, and only one of them
is a length. The formatter does not throw; the reason is rendered where the figure would have
been:

#!fsharp

let temperature = t |> Array.map (fun t -> 293.0<K> + 0.5<K/s> * t)

Chart.lineIn U.second U.metre t x
|> Chart.withName "displacement"
|> Chart.addLineIn U.second U.kelvin t temperature
|> Chart.withName "temperature"

#!markdown

That error is not a failed conversion — the arrays are separately well typed. It is the one
dimensional mistake a compiler cannot catch, reported when the figure is compiled, with both layers
named.

## The same figure, as TeX

`Theme.tikz` swaps the emitter's target and the unit spelling together: axis labels become
`siunitx` commands, so the units are typeset by the same package that typesets the rest of the
paper, in the document's own fonts.

#!fsharp

#r "nuget: Marey.Backend.Tikz"

let figure =
    Chart.lineIn U.second U.metre t x
    |> Chart.withName "model"
    |> Chart.withTitle "Free fall"
    |> Chart.withTheme Theme.tikz

Chart.render TikzBackend.render (Chart.size 320.0 200.0) figure
|> fun tex -> tex.Split('\n') |> Array.filter (fun l -> l.Contains "qty") |> String.concat "\n"

#!markdown

`\qty{}{\second}` and `\qty{}{\metre}`, from the unit witnesses, with nothing configured but the
theme. Paste the full output into a LaTeX document between `\begin{document}` and `\end{document}`
and it compiles.

## The colour system

Palettes are checked for contrast and colour-vision-deficiency safety. Colours, ramps and palettes
all display, so the validator's answer is readable rather than a record to be squinted at.

#!fsharp

Palette.okabeIto

#!fsharp

Palette.validate Color.white Palette.okabeIto

#!markdown

Four vision models, the worst one in bold, and the two entries that were closest under it.

The report reads `FAILS`. Okabe–Ito stays distinguishable under all three dichromacies — every
separation clears the floor — but two of its eight colours are very light, and against a *white*
background its yellow lands at 1.32:1, far below WCAG's 3:1 for graphical objects. The palette is
not modified, since a corrected Okabe–Ito is no longer Okabe–Ito; it is reordered. `Palette.series`
demotes the yellow to index 6 and the black to index 7, so seven series are drawn before either is
reached, and the dark theme swaps the black for white outright.

That reordering is what makes a *theme* pass where the raw set does not:

#!fsharp

Palette.validate Theme.dark.PanelBackground Palette.seriesDark

#!markdown

`Ok`, weakest contrast 3.15:1 — the same eight hues with the black swapped out, on the background
they are actually drawn on. Both results are pinned by the test suite.

The sequential ramps are perceptually ordered in Oklab, and the bars below are resampled *through*
Oklab rather than handed to the browser as stops: a CSS gradient interpolates in sRGB, which is the
artefact these ramps exist to avoid.

#!fsharp

Palette.viridis

#!fsharp

Palette.diverging

#!markdown

Blue to yellow shows the difference without measurement: the perceptual path goes through a green,
the sRGB one through a dead grey.

#!fsharp

Ramp.ofColors "blue-yellow" [| Palette.blue; Palette.yellow |]

#!markdown

## Below the front door

The three types the rest of the library is built out of also display, which is how a figure that
came out wrong is inspected.

A physical dimension, in Core's fallback spelling and in the SI unit `Marey.Units` derives from it:

#!fsharp

Dim.sub Dim.length (Dim.pow 2 Dim.time)

#!markdown

A unit witness — the runtime value that carries a measure's meaning. Degrees Celsius is the
interesting one: affine, so its conversion to SI is not a factor alone.

#!fsharp

U.degreeCelsius

#!markdown

An erased scale: the axis exactly as a backend sees it, after the measure is gone and a `Dim` has
taken its place.

#!fsharp

Erase.scale (Scale.log10 U.metre (1.0<m>, 1000.0<m>))

#!markdown

And the IR itself. Ten cases, with a census of how much of it there is:

#!fsharp

Chart.toScene (Chart.size 480.0 300.0) figure

#!markdown

## Motion

A `Film` is an animation: a scene at every instant, and the one layout every frame is drawn
inside. The cell **plays** it — sampled into stills, stacked in one SVG and cycled, with no
encoder installed and nothing written to disk.

The layout is computed once for the whole shot, which is why the panel holds still while the data
moves under it.

#!fsharp

open Marey.Storyboard

let ringdown =
    let t = Array.init 60 (fun i -> float i * 0.1<s>)
    let x = t |> Array.map (fun v -> 30.0e-3 * exp (-float v / 2.0) * cos (4.0 * float v) * 1.0<m>)
    Chart.scatterIn U.second U.metre t x |> Chart.withTitle "A ringdown"

let story =
    scene {
        let! trace = draw ringdown |> over 0.8<s>
        do! trace |> zoomToY (-0.008, 0.008) |> over 1.2<s> |> ease Ease.cubicInOut
    }

Film.ofStory 32 (Chart.size 480.0 300.0) story

#!markdown

`Html.film` samples 48 frames by default — a budget rather than a frame rate, since each frame is
about 11 KB of markup. `Html.filmAt` takes the count when a longer film needs a different trade.

#!markdown

## Sizing

Figures are compiled to fit the cell. Verso hands the formatter the available width and height, so
nothing is guessed — resize the window, re-run a cell, and the figure follows, clamped to a sane
band at both ends.

For an explicit size, render to SVG and let Verso's built-in SVG formatter draw it. That path needs
**no extension**: `SvgFormatter` claims any string beginning with `<svg`, which is what the SVG
backend produces.

#!fsharp

Chart.render SvgBackend.render (Chart.size 1000.0 380.0) figure

#!markdown

That is also the general fallback: on a machine where the Marey extension is not installed, every
figure above still renders when piped through `Chart.render SvgBackend.render (Chart.size w h)`.
